ular  person who kind of creates a model and  then as that person either fades out of  a situation or whatever things fall  apart I don't think the district is  scalable mm-hmm so I think the question  we should be it's actually we have proof  right at least in New York City that the  scaling didn't work very well right  right  we've got in New York State we've close  to a million kids who failed the test  I don't know under what measure that  could be a successful scaling experience  so maybe the model is not one giant  system maybe the model is lots of social  entrepreneurs who have school districts  of varying sizes you know that's why I  support everything from tax credits to  charters to you know bold district  reform you know we got to try a bunch of  things and we need to see if we can get  real talent into the space critics of  charters argue that schools like Success  Academy are excelling in part because  they serve kids that come from more  stable homes with more involved parents  than their public school counterparts  those parents that are willing to take  the effort to enter their kids into a  lottery critics say or more likely to  have children who are motivated to get  ahead is there a difference between the  parents who decide to go into a lottery  so you know 18,000 kids are apply for  your spaces are the parents of those  18,000 kids somehow radically different  than you know the parents who either  don't care don't know about lack of a  school I this is sort of an unknowable  question but I spend an enormous amount  of time with parents I did before I open  the first school as an elected official  going to communities I did more parent  PTA meetings hundreds and hundreds of  meetings and I have met very few parents  who do not deeply deeply love and care  for their children  I think it's on the school to educate  and do outreach to parents we get  criticized a lot for marketing and we  mark it because we want to make sure  that parents understand